  "page": "02_photosynthesis",
  "spoken": true,
  "problems": [],
  "old_rules_spoken": true,
  "text": "This page explains photosynthesis, which is how green plants make their own food in the leaves. The process involves chlorophyll trapping light energy from the sun. Plants take in carbon dioxide from the air and water from the soil to produce glucose (food) while releasing oxygen. The equation for this process is carbon dioxide plus water results in glucose and oxygen with the use of light energy and chlorophyll.\n\nIn an experimental setup, a leaf kept in darkness for a certain period (represented by the marker 48 hours) showed a negative starch test. This implies that without sunlight, the leaf could not perform photosynthesis effectively, resulting in no production of starch.",
  "unsafe": []
 },
 {
  "model": "magana-lora",
  "lang": "en",
  "page": "02_photosynthesis",
  "spoken": true,
  "problems": [],
  "old_rules_spoken": true,
  "text": "This page explains photosynthesis. Green plants make their own food in the leaves with the help of chlorophyll, which traps light energy from the sun. They take in carbon dioxide from the air and water from the soil, and produce glucose while giving out oxygen. The process uses light energy and chlorophyll, and can be represented by the equation carbon dioxide + water -> glucose + oxygen. In an experiment, a leaf kept in the dark for 48 hours gave a negative starch test.",
  "unsafe": []
 },
